<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
    <!--
    ReorientCycle: one full pick -> rotate -> place-at-zone-center cycle,
    bringing whichever face is currently up to `target`. Reused once for
    an adjacent-face target, or twice (via an intermediate face) for an
    opposite-face target -- see MainTree below.

    The lift+rotate move is attempted at progressively larger lift heights
    (Fallback: 0.15 -> 0.22 -> 0.32 -> 0.45m), combined lift+rotation in a
    single MoveToPose per level rather than two separate moves. This
    matters for two reasons: (1) a genuine reachability failure (not the
    transient motion_server IK-timeout bug -- see TESTING.md / project
    memory) is deterministic, retrying the exact same pose gives the exact
    same NO_IK_SOLUTION every time, so more clearance from the table is
    what actually resolves it, not repetition; (2) splitting lift and
    rotate into two separate moves let an earlier version of this tree
    partially succeed (lift moves the arm) then fail (rotate doesn't), so
    the *next* Fallback branch's "relative" lift stacked on top of the
    already-executed one instead of starting clean -- observed compounding
    into an ever-taller, ever-more-unreachable pose. A single combined
    move either fully succeeds or leaves the arm exactly where it started
    (MoveIt doesn't execute a plan it couldn't find), so each escalation
    level is a true independent attempt. A true wedge (SEND_GOAL_TIMEOUT)
    is not retried here at all -- that's handled by run_bt.sh restarting
    the whole robot stack.

    Tried cartesian_motion="true" on the lift+rotate move to get smoother,
    more predictable motion (reported live as the arm visibly "flailing"
    between escalation levels with joint-space planning, since each
    independent OMPL plan has no continuity guarantee and can land on a
    very different configuration -- elbow flip, wrist reconfiguration --
    than the previous attempt). Reverted: a combined ~90 degree
    reorientation plus translation is usually infeasible as a single
    straight-line Cartesian path, so cartesian_motion="true" here failed
    fast and consistently (FAILURE, not even NO_IK_SOLUTION) instead of
    the escalation ever getting a chance to work. Kept cartesian_motion
    "false" (joint-space) for lift+rotate, but added velocity_scaling to
    slow it down -- doesn't fix the underlying discontinuity between
    escalation attempts, but keeps whatever reconfiguration does happen
    visibly controlled rather than sudden. cartesian_motion="true" is
    kept for the final placement move (a pure translation, no rotation,
    which straight-line planning handles fine) and bypasses
    motion_server's buggy _compute_ik path there as a bonus (see
    TESTING.md / project memory).
    -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="ReorientCycle">
        <Sequence>
            <!-- Defensive release: if the PREVIOUS ReorientCycle attempt
                 (e.g. the direct-rotation try, before a flip_grasp/tilt_deg
                 fallback) failed mid-rotation, it exits FAILURE with the
                 die still attached and the gripper still closed -- there's
                 no detach/open on the failure path, only on success.
                 Detach/open here first, wherever the arm currently is, so
                 the die settles back to the table under it before we read
                 its face; ignore DetachObject failure (Fallback +
                 AlwaysSuccess) since on the normal path nothing is
                 attached yet and that's not an error.
                 No MoveToJoint-to-home here anymore: MainTree already
                 homes before the first ReorientCycle call, and repeating
                 it on every single call (direct attempt, then again for
                 every flip_grasp/tilt_deg/detour fallback) was pure
                 wasted time when the arm was already there. The one place
                 a fresh-process wedge was ever observed is the very FIRST
                 MoveToPose/MoveToJoint after bt_executer_node starts up
                 (see TESTING.md / project memory) -- MainTree's own home
                 move already covers that once per run. -->
            <Fallback>
                <DetachObject service_name="/detach_object" object_id="dice"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.045" max_effort="1.0"/>
            <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face}" pose="{dice_pose}"/>
            <!-- GetFaceRotation moved up here, before the pick, from where
                 it used to sit (right before the rotate move): it only
                 needs current_face/target_face -- both already known --
                 via TF lookups on the die's own face{N}_tf frames, which
                 don't depend on the robot having touched the die at all.
                 Computing it now lets GetGraspOrientation see the
                 rotation it's about to be asked to perform and pick a
                 tilt sign that agrees with it (see that class's
                 comment) -- and conveniently means the SAME rel_quan/
                 ref_frame get reused for the actual rotate move further
                 down instead of looking it up twice. -->
            <GetFaceRotation current_face="{face}" target_face="{target}" orientation="{rel_quat}" ref_frame="{ref_frame}"/>
            <GetGraspOrientation current_face="{face}" target_face="{target}" flip_grasp="{flip_grasp}" tilt_deg="{tilt_deg}" rotation_quat="{rel_quat}" pick_orientation="{pick_orientation}" grasp_yaw_deg="{grasp_yaw}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" cartesian_motion="false" frame_id="dice_tf" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{pick_orientation}"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>
            <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.0" max_effort="1.0"/>
            <AttachObject service_name="/attach_object" object_id="dice" target_frame_id="tool0"/>
            <!-- Relocate to the safe zone (away from the camera bar --
                 see the placement-target comment below) BEFORE rotating,
                 not just at the end: the die can spawn anywhere in the
                 challenge's allowed area, including right under the bar,
                 and the rotation itself (not just the final placement)
                 fails there. Using dice_pose (captured before any lift,
                 at the original DiceIdentification above) rather than a
                 post-lift reading, since a pure XY correction doesn't
                 care about height. z_offset=0.10 lifts by that fixed
                 clearance WHILE translating, combined into the single
                 move below -- unlike lift+ROTATE (which reliably fails to
                 find a straight-line Cartesian path for large
                 reorientations, why those stay two separate moves),
                 lift+TRANSLATE is just a 3D straight line, no orientation
                 change involved, so combining them is safe and saves a
                 whole extra planning+execution round-trip.
                 Same (0.45, 0.1) as the final placement target below, by
                 design: rotating in place only drifts the die a little
                 (a few cm, since the rotation pivot isn't exactly the
                 die's center) -- deliberately using the same target for
                 both means the post-rotation ComputeXYCorrection further
                 down only has that small drift plus the release descent
                 left to do, in one move, instead of a whole separate trip
                 to a different spot. -->
            <ComputeXYCorrection current_pose="{dice_pose}" target_x="0.45" target_y="0.1" z_offset="0.10" xy_correction="{xy_corr_pre}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="true" frame_id="base_link" position="{xy_corr_pre}" orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>
            <!-- No separate baseline lift before rotating anymore: the
                 relocation move just above already leaves the die 0.10m
                 up (z_offset), which is already at the bottom of the
                 0.10-0.18m range historically found necessary to avoid
                 NO_IK_SOLUTION on a pure in-place rotation (see
                 ReorientCycle's header history) -- stacking another 0.20m
                 on top of that was needless height (and an extra
                 planning+execution round-trip) once the relocation lift
                 already covers it. rel_quat/ref_frame already computed
                 above (before the pick), reused here as-is: purely
                 orientation-based, unaffected by the die having moved. -->
            <!-- Two height-escalation levels only (was five, one a
                 copy-paste duplicate): a joint-limit failure doesn't
                 respond to more clearance margin (verified empirically up
                 to 0.50m -- see grasp-angle fallbacks in MainTree, which
                 are what actually addresses that failure mode), so extra
                 height levels here only burn run_bt.sh's wall-clock
                 budget across the now-several grasp-angle variants without
                 improving the odds. Kept two levels for genuine
                 clearance-limited cases on other face pairs. -->
            <Fallback>
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="true" frame_id="{ref_frame}" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{rel_quat}"/>
                <Sequence>
                    <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="false" velocity_scaling="0.4" frame_id="{ref_frame}" position="0.0;0.0;0.15" orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0"/>
                    <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="false" velocity_scaling="0.4" frame_id="{ref_frame}" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{rel_quat}"/>
                </Sequence>
            </Fallback>
            <!-- skip_placement (required on every caller, see MainTree):
                 true for the INTERMEDIATE hop of a two-cycle (opposite-
                 face) rotation only. That hop's placement is thrown away
                 almost immediately -- the second cycle re-identifies and
                 re-picks the die from wherever it landed regardless -- so
                 the precise XY+Z correction below is both wasted work and
                 (observed empirically) a real source of failure: it can
                 leave the arm in a post-rotation wrist configuration from
                 which that correction has NO reachable solution at all
                 (cartesian AND joint-space both failed, NO_IK_SOLUTION),
                 with no further fallback at the MainTree level for
                 num_steps==2 to recover from. Skipping it here means the
                 intermediate hop just opens the gripper and lets the die
                 drop/settle wherever, which is fine since nothing reads
                 its position precisely until the second cycle's own
                 identification. Fallback structure: the Precondition
                 reports FAILURE outright (without even trying its child)
                 when skip_placement is false, so the outer Fallback moves
                 on to the full precise-placement branch; when true, it
                 runs the simplified release and the Fallback stops there. -->
            <Fallback>
                <Precondition if="skip_placement == 'true'" else="FAILURE">
                    <Sequence>
                        <Fallback>
                            <DetachObject service_name="/detach_object" object_id="dice"/>
                            <AlwaysSuccess/>
                        </Fallback>
                        <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.045" max_effort="1.0"/>
                    </Sequence>
                </Precondition>
                <Sequence>
                    <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face2}" pose="{dice_pose2}"/>
                    <!-- (0.45, 0.1) instead of the originally-chosen (0.6,
                         0.1): the cell has a real camera-mount crossbar
                         (collision geometry in the SRDF, not just visual)
                         whose shadow covers a wider stretch of the table
                         than a single point -- confirmed empirically (see
                         project memory / TESTING.md) that x=0.6 fails even
                         at y=-0.15, while (0.45, 0.1) is clear and
                         reachable directly. Lowering the lift height does
                         NOT help clearing the bar (tested down to 0.08m,
                         still fails) -- the interference is the arm's own
                         wrist/forearm sweep needed for that orientation,
                         not the die/gripper height, so only moving
                         sideways, out from under the bar, actually fixes
                         it.
                         target_z=0.027 (base_link) is the die's own
                         measured resting height (TF: base_link ->
                         dice_tf when sitting flat on the table) --
                         correcting straight to this known value in the
                         SAME move as the XY correction, rather than
                         trying to mirror each intermediate lift (the
                         pre-rotation relocation's 0.10m clearance lift,
                         the baseline 0.20m rotation lift, ...) with a
                         matching descent by hand: that approach was tried
                         and left the die released ~0.3m above the table,
                         because those lifts don't cleanly cancel out (the
                         escalation branch adds its own on top, the
                         relocation lift is separate again) -- correcting
                         to a known absolute height, exactly like
                         target_x/target_y already do, is robust
                         regardless of which lifts happened on the way. -->
                    <ComputeXYCorrection current_pose="{dice_pose2}" target_x="0.45" target_y="0.1" target_z="0.027" xy_correction="{xy_corr}"/>
                    <!-- Fallback, not RetryUntilSuccessful: observed this
                         combined XY+Z correction fail deterministically
                         (ACTION_ABORTED) from certain post-rotation wrist
                         configurations -- retrying the identical cartesian
                         move just repeats the same failure. Falls back to
                         joint-space planning, the same escalation strategy
                         already used for the rotation step above, rather
                         than a plain retry of an unreachable straight-line
                         path. -->
                    <Fallback>
                        <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="true" frame_id="base_link" position="{xy_corr}" orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0"/>
                        <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="false" velocity_scaling="0.4" frame_id="base_link" position="{xy_corr}" orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0"/>
                    </Fallback>
                    <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.045" max_effort="1.0"/>
                    <DetachObject service_name="/detach_object" object_id="dice"/>
                </Sequence>
            </Fallback>
        </Sequence>
    </BehaviorTree>

    <!--
    MainTree: the real challenge entry point. Edit target_face below (the
    one and only place it needs to be set) to change which face the die
    should end up showing.
    -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
        <Sequence>
            <Script code="target_face:=6" />  <!-- STRESS_TEST_TARGET -->
            <!-- Same defensive release ReorientCycle does at its own
                 start, but here at the MainTree entry point too: if
                 num_steps ends up 0 (die already showing target_face),
                 ReorientCycle is never called at all, so its own
                 defensive detach/open would never run -- silently
                 reporting success while a die left attached/mid-air from
                 a previous crashed/interrupted run gets read as if it
                 were genuinely resting and showing that face. Observed
                 directly: a leftover attached die's in-air orientation
                 happened to read as a plausible face, and the tree
                 reported "no errors" while the die was still in the
                 gripper the whole time. -->
            <Fallback>
                <DetachObject service_name="/detach_object" object_id="dice"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.045" max_effort="1.0"/>
            <MoveToJoint action_name="/move_to_joint" joint_target="0.0;-1.97;2.13;-1.83;-1.50;0.0"/>
            <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face}" pose="{dice_pose}"/>
            <PlanRotationPath current_face="{face}" target_face="{target_face}" num_steps="{num_steps}" intermediate_face="{intermediate}" detour_face="{detour}"/>
            <Fallback>
                <Precondition if="num_steps==0" else="FAILURE">
                    <AlwaysSuccess/>
                </Precondition>
                <Precondition if="num_steps==1" else="FAILURE">
                    <!-- Direct rotation first, DEFAULT grasp -- which is
                         now tilt_deg=-45 (see GetGraspOrientation): the
                         wrist tilts forward, in the direction of reach,
                         verified empirically to reliably clear the
                         post-pick rotation. If it still fails entirely
                         (exhausts ReorientCycle's own escalation) for some
                         other face pair, escalate through other wrist
                         configurations reaching the *same* physical grasp
                         (same face pair pinched, so target_face safety is
                         unaffected): yaw flipped 180 degrees (still at the
                         default -45 tilt), then the plain vertical grasp
                         (tilt_deg=0, the previous default), then tilted
                         the *other* way (+45 -- reliably fails this same
                         rotation even with a clear, obstacle-free path,
                         kept only as a last-ditch option before the
                         detour). Only as a true last resort, route through
                         detour_face instead: two differently-axed 90
                         degree rotations replacing the one that's stuck. -->
                    <Fallback>
                        <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{target_face}" skip_placement="false"/>
                        <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{target_face}" flip_grasp="true" skip_placement="false"/>
                        <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{target_face}" tilt_deg="0.0" skip_placement="false"/>
                        <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{target_face}" tilt_deg="45.0" skip_placement="false"/>
                        <Sequence>
                            <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{detour}" skip_placement="true"/>
                            <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{target_face}" skip_placement="false"/>
                        </Sequence>
                    </Fallback>
                </Precondition>
                <Precondition if="num_steps==2" else="FAILURE">
                    <Sequence>
                        <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{intermediate}" skip_placement="true"/>
                        <SubTree ID="ReorientCycle" target="{target_face}" skip_placement="false"/>
                    </Sequence>
                </Precondition>
            </Fallback>
        </Sequence>
    </BehaviorTree>
</root>
